.TH ALSP 4
.SH NAME
alsp, Def, Type, Refs, Impls, Doc, Comp, Syms, Diag, Fmt, Rename, Act \- acme's windows in a language server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B alsp
[
.B -D
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.PP
.B Def
.PP
.B Type
.PP
.B Refs
.PP
.B Impls
.PP
.B Doc
.PP
.B Comp
.PP
.B Syms
.PP
.B Diag
.PP
.B Fmt
.PP
.B Rename
.I name
.PP
.B Act
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Alsp
puts the text of
.IR acme (1)'s
windows in
.IR lspfs (4),
and the tokens and diagnostics
.I lspfs
gives back in each window's
.IR astyles (4)
layers.
It posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
.RB ( /srv/alsp )
and mounts itself on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/alsp ),
then waits for
.BR $acmesrv ,
an acme's
.B /srv
file, and follows its windows.
.B Acmestart
starts it after
.I astyles
and
.IR lspfs ,
before acme.
.PP
A window whose file the first
.B file=
of the rules in
.B $home/lib/lsp
or
.B /lib/lsp
matches is opened in
.IR lspfs ,
and each edit to it is sent there, as acme's log gives it.
The edits are counted, as acme counts them, so each thing
.I alsp
reads or writes is at a known place in the window's history:
the text of an insert too long for acme's log is read under the edit's
.B seq
guard (see
.IR astyles (4)),
and if the window has moved on,
.I alsp
reads the whole body again and sends it.
.I Get
sends the body again.
Tokens go in the window's layer
.BR lsp ,
diagnostics in
.BR diagnostics ,
each written under the guard of the last edit sent, and again when it was refused for a newer one.
.PP
After a
.IR Put ,
.I alsp
does what the rule's
.B put=
says:
.L fmt
lays the window out, by
.IR lspfs 's
.BR fmt ,
as one change, so one
.I Undo
takes it back; the window is left dirty.
A format of text that changed meanwhile is not applied.
Writing
.BI fmt " winid"
to
.B ctl
does the same at once.
.PP
Writing
.BI doc " winid"
to
.B ctl
writes what
.IR lspfs 's
.B doc
gives at the window's dot into the window
.B +Doc
of its file's directory, made if need be, as
.I Doc
does. While that window is open, each time the file's dot rests
(acme's
.B S
line in the window's
.BR log ),
alsp writes what is at dot there, unless the answer is empty.
.PP
Writing
.BI review " edit dir"
to
.B ctl
reads an edit in
.IR lspfs 's
form, as
.B rename
gives it, from the file
.IR edit ,
and writes the text of each file it touches, as its window or the disk has it, under
.IB dir /a1,
and the text it would leave under
.IB dir /b1,
each at its full name.
Writing
.BI apply " edit"
applies it.
Nothing is done if any file's text changed since the edit was asked for, or a file to be made
exists.
Each file's edits go in its window, as one change, and a file with no window is opened in one;
the windows are left dirty, for a
.I Put
to write.
A file renamed is renamed on disk, and its window with it; a file deleted is removed from disk,
its window left dirty; a file created is a new window, dirty and empty.
.PP
The commands work on the window they are run in, at dot:
.I Def
and
.I Type
plumb the definition of the word or of its type;
.I Refs
and
.I Impls
list the places the word is used and implemented, each with its function, by
.IR func (1);
.I Doc
gives the diagnostics on the line, then what the server says of the word,
in the window
.B +Doc
of the file's directory; while that window is open, it follows the file's dot,
and shows what is at dot whenever dot rests;
.I Comp
lists the completions at dot;
.I Syms
lists the file's symbols;
.I Diag
lists its diagnostics;
.I Fmt
lays it out;
.I Rename
renames the word, everywhere the server knows it;
and
.I Act
lists the server's actions for dot in a window
.BR +Act ,
each name with its title, where button 2 on a name picks it.
The lists are lines that button 3 opens.
.B Lspshow
shows a rename, or an action's edit, before it applies, in a window
.B +Rename
or
.B +Act
of the file operations and a
.IR diff (1)
of each file;
.I Apply
in its tag applies it, and runs the action's command if it has one.
.SH FILES
.TF /mnt/alsp/ctl
.TP
.B /mnt/alsp/ctl
.BI fmt " winid" \f1,
.BI doc " winid" \f1,
.BI review " edit dir" \f1,
.BI apply " edit"
.PD
